[[:db/retract 17592186089970 :answer-field/choices 17592186089972] [:db/retract 17592186089970 :answer-field/choices 17592186089973] {:db/id 17592186092190 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ea263a27-2e68-51ff-b5e7-5e8c84ebe1bc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092191 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-22c3e851-1c76-57c4-8e64-d220300f1c79" "question-1bc13b0e-7f8a-5b76-a219-9d833008d899"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186413946 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n${3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case {{field-1}}.\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}>{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$\n{{field-4}},\nwe have\n\n$$\n{{field-5}}>{{field-6}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, ${3}^{n}>1+{2}^{n}$ for all $n\\ge 2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186413945 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\le {{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$\n{{field-3}},\nwe have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\\le {{field-5}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}\\le {{field-7}}\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $(n+2)^{2}\\le {3}^{n+1}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea263a27-2e68-51ff-b5e7-5e8c84ebe1bc" :question/id #uuid "ea263a27-2e68-51ff-b5e7-5e8c84ebe1bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-182453" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\\ge {{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{3}+(k+1)^{2} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{3}+{k}^{2}+3{k}^{2}+5k+2 & \\ge {{field-10}} \\\\  & ={{field-11}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\ge {{field-12}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ is true for all\n{{field-13}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3}+{1}^{2}=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}+{k}^{2}\\ge 2k.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^{3}+(k+1)^{2}\\ge 2(k+1).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{3}+(k+1)^{2} & =({k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+3k+1)+({k}^{2}+2k+1) \\\\  & ={k}^{3}+{k}^{2}+3{k}^{2}+5k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{k}^{3}+{k}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 2k}+{{3{k}^{2}+5k}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+2 & \\ge 2k+2=2(k+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fff2f547-9756-58c3-a5cc-c52c8137a0aa" "answer-field-4284beb9-a84c-507c-b0a5-b067e447b217" "answer-field-fe2d977b-8949-5cee-8dda-208d58987d96" "answer-field-ad580862-74a1-57d5-9142-8448c19d5d01" "answer-field-1216ef77-e331-5d2d-a6bc-ad5097411412" "answer-field-af7c23c4-d4da-5383-b80e-f2adb93254f7" "answer-field-65c0f0ef-2cec-5fac-ae44-e78d87f6a28a" "answer-field-fa7dfeff-d1d0-5621-8852-e1c6ac8957e9" "answer-field-fd7b07b5-24fc-59ba-b8f9-b367006e4d40" "answer-field-3152f665-862f-5816-a010-247b4923f7af" "answer-field-1446d5e1-c7ea-5263-838d-ea1add9d5a63" "answer-field-2109c8db-0af7-57e6-b5b4-f0d1f686475e" "answer-field-79c4d566-447f-509b-98d1-fc84890cc450"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/i