-0aabb323-bbd9-5f8f-a974-350a80be3297" :answer/id #uuid "0aabb323-bbd9-5f8f-a974-350a80be3297" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{k}^{3}+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3b06450e-1106-561c-9b62-a91a003e80ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b06450e-1106-561c-9b62-a91a003e80ff" :answer-field/key "field-14" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-96fa12e3-4f2d-5246-b8a4-d59a0feeef23" :answer-field/choices ["answer-274e80a6-2919-570f-b630-4b63f202d96f" "answer-2febb805-9b7b-51f3-b3b3-e3f8abe7f679" "answer-96fa12e3-4f2d-5246-b8a4-d59a0feeef23" "answer-661b83ff-5881-56c5-af55-9356963331fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-274e80a6-2919-570f-b630-4b63f202d96f" :answer/id #uuid "274e80a6-2919-570f-b630-4b63f202d96f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2febb805-9b7b-51f3-b3b3-e3f8abe7f679" :answer/id #uuid "2febb805-9b7b-51f3-b3b3-e3f8abe7f679" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n>4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96fa12e3-4f2d-5246-b8a4-d59a0feeef23" :answer/id #uuid "96fa12e3-4f2d-5246-b8a4-d59a0feeef23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n\\ge 3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-661b83ff-5881-56c5-af55-9356963331fc" :answer/id #uuid "661b83ff-5881-56c5-af55-9356963331fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "n\\le 4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22c3e851-1c76-57c4-8e64-d220300f1c79" :question/id #uuid "22c3e851-1c76-57c4-8e64-d220300f1c79" :question/math-academy-id "q-183366" :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-9}} & ={{field-10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {2}^{k+1} & >{{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & >{{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{2}^{n+1}>n+1\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{1+1}=4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+1}>k+1.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{2}^{k+2}>k+2.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{(k+1)+1} & ={2}^{k+1}\\cdot {2}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {2}^{k+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {{{2}^{k+1}}_{⏟}}_{>\\,k+1} & >2(k+1) \\\\  & =2k+2 \\\\  & =k+2+{{k}_{⏟}}_{>\\,0} \\\\  & >k+2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${2}^{n+1}>n+1$ for all $n\\ge 1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c5bfee5-f006-5b94-abd3-10f19910750f" "answer-field-23c17b2c-d033-572e-8860-06735fdaba95" "answer-field-7b706d34-d24a-5725-bac5-bc5d1aea746c" "answer-field-60444a6e-3f7a-5005-be36-d92b6b3e4982" "answer-field-b461a8da-3a5e-5bb3-a765-04376d630ad7" "answer-field-7ad52ed3-ec6b-5d90-ba2e-8057e21ceea0" "answer-field-b8cf8de4-e854-53cc-9c91-3519c91f8f97" "answer-field-bfa77cfa-58e4-5b25-ae91-85c90631ea73" "answer-field-7ee17ac5-5956-5a60-9fef-14fd91096637" "answer-field-18d7c5f3-bd44-583b-9c32-df8890dca7dd" "answer-field-114b6510-9437-5ad4-bf81-e6a2ad477c7e" "answer-field-47e251cc-c2b0-5d16-9b1a-1d2a92c010e0" "answer-field-a63b6ac9-60db-5b6f-9cf3-213766175f89" "answer-field-055318bb-3511-5881-871b-f99c8b002fc7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c5bfee5-f006-5b94-abd3-10f19910750f" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c5bfee5-f006-5b94-abd3-10f19910750f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-bdd1a43d-bd9e-50d3-b4ea-1514909908fd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-adc9a0b6-cef8-5400-b408-fa2275422678" "answer-bdd1a43d-bd9e-50d3-b4ea-1514909908fd" "answer-e89863d0-e9b0-5de5-8fa2-8e1358a9d272"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-